00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.190
PROFESSOR: Welcome back to 8701.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.400
In the second chapter, we
will discuss symmetries

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.270
and the importance of symmetries
in physics in general,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.780
but also especially in particle
physics and nuclear physics.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.930
So we start with a short
introductory video,

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.680
and then we'll move on to
more details as we go along.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.230
The importance of symmetries
cannot be understated

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.730
in physics.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.780
And there's two aspects
which are important.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.710
The first one is that
symmetries and conservation

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.960
laws go hand in hand, as
discussed by Noether's theorem.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.900
To express the theorem
in an informal way,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.430
you can say that if a system
has a continuous symmetry

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.610
property, then there are
corresponding properties whose

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:06.640
values do not change with time,
meaning that they're conserved.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.180
You can express this
more sophisticated,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.020
and say to every differentiable
symmetry generated

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.300
by local action,
there's correspondence.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.500
There's a correspondent
conserved current.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.940
And we're going to look at
those actions and currents

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.130
as we go along.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.050
The second aspect,
beyond the fact

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.240
that there's
conservation laws, is

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.490
that you can understand
physics experiments and nature

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.390
if you know that physics
has an underlying symmetry,

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.560
without fully
understanding the physics

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.290
or the mathematical
backgrounds in order

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.760
to do calculation in detail.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.860
So knowing that there
is underlying symmetry

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:53.010
can help in really expressing
or understanding the physics

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:57.190
behavior of experiments.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.230
A few historic remarks
on Emmy Noether--

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:07.280
Emmy Noether was born in Germany
in the 1880s in Erlangen,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.060
where she grew up
and also studied

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:14.470
Mathematics at the University
of Erlangen. After getting

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.660
her degree, she worked
for a full seven years

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.810
at the university in
the Math Department,

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.900
and received zero
dollars, and not

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.660
just because it wasn't the
currency being used there,

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.810
but at that time,
women didn't really

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.970
have a prominent
role in academia.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.480
And so there was no
job for her to take.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.330
But her talents and
her qualification

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:45.640
was seen in the mathematical
world at the time,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.820
specifically in the center
of the mathematical world,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.020
which was in Goettingen.
So Hilbert basically

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:56.770
discovered her, and asked her
to come to Goettingen. In order

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.700
to do habilitation, she did get
an habilitation in Goettingen

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.120
in 1919, and then
stayed in Goettingen

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:09.550
till the situation in Europe
degraded in the 1930s.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:15.400
She was born Jewish and
couldn't stay in Goettingen

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.980
beyond the year
1933, and then had

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.200
to immigrate into
the United States,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:26.290
where she worked at
Bryn Mawr College,

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:29.380
and also with Princeton.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:34.390
Her work-- you see here
her habilitation, which

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.340
is in German
[SPEAKING GERMAN],, "Invariant

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:43.510
Variation of Problems,"
was highly regarded.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.670
And she had a lot of
influence and impact

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:51.390
on various strands of
mathematics and physics.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.090
Unfortunately, she
passed away already

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.900
when she was about 50 years old.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.440
She was diagnosed with
some sort of cancer,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.170
and passed away really,
really quickly after this,

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.120
after some surgery.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.480
Her temperature rose and a few
days later, she passed away.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.820
To come back to symmetries
and conservation laws,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.149
every symmetry of nature
uses a conservation law.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.700
That is what Noether's
theorem tells you.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.230
And you can reverse
this to saying

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.570
that every conservation
law in physics

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.910
reflects an underlying symmetry.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:31.930
And examples for
this are the fact

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.750
that the properties,
the laws of physics

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:38.320
are invariant on the
time translation, meaning

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.290
that physics is the same
yesterday, the same tomorrow,

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.210
and it's going to be
the same next week.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:46.780
And out of this, we can
deduce energy conservation.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.120
Similarly, translation
in space results

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.100
in a momentum
conservation, angular

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.220
rotations or rotations
without the angular momentum.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.900
And then a little
bit harder to grasp,

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.780
but we will see
this in more detail,

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:06.310
internal symmetries can also
lead to conservation laws.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:10.840
And gauge transformation leads
to the conservation of charge.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:16.240
So there is internal
symmetries as well.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.880
Before we dive into more
detail, a few things.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.700
First, in many cases,
symmetry operations

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:29.260
can be expressed via
matrices or groups.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:36.400
And there's a few
rules or operations

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.230
which are rather important
and define symmetry.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.290
The first one is that
any symmetry operation

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.780
has to have identity,
meaning there

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.160
has to be an operation
which doesn't do anything

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.590
with an element of this group.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:57.040
There has to be closure,
meaning that if you apply

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.620
a first transformation
and then a second,

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:03.610
the resulting transformation
is, again, part

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:06.130
of the set of transformations.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.460
And there is an inverse, meaning
that if you rotate in one

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.100
direction, you can rotate back.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.570
And there's
associativity, meaning

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:21.250
that if you have a rotation
acting on two other rotations,

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.840
you can regroup and follow
what's shown in this equation

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.600
here.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.310
It's not clear that
you can reverse

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:37.020
the order of certain elements
of your group or your symmetry

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.610
operation.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.380
You can classify them, however.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.760
Those where you can commute,
those are called abelian

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.860
groups, and those that you
cannot, those are non-abelian.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:55.165
All right, so with this,
we have introduced,

00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:57.900
with the first
video, symmetries.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.030
And now, we just
dive into more detail

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.250
in understanding continuous
symmetries and also

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.530
discrete symmetries, and
what we can learn from them.

